this is jeff bezos at 16 years old he  was flipping burgers at mcdonald's in  2021 he became the richest man on the  planet with a net worth of more than 200  billion dollars but it hasn't been all  smooth sailing bezos began his career in  the hedge fund world in the 1990s then  left a cushy job to launch his own  startup that didn't turn a profit for  years in 2018 he weathered a  high-profile divorce scandal and amazon  has faced scrutiny over how it treats  its workers he's since stepped down as  amazon ceo and has been pursuing other  ventures from launching into space  aboard the blue origin rocket to  amazon's domination in various sectors  bezos's conquest to rule the world is  becoming a reality this is how he went  from mcdonald's employee to hedge fund  manager to the richest man on the planet  [Music]  he was born on january 12 1964. his  mother jackie got divorced from her  first husband and married cuban  immigrant miguel bezos who adopted jeff  when bezos was four his mother told his  biological father to stay out of their  lives bezos showed signs of brilliance  from an early age when he was a toddler  he took apart his crib with a  screwdriver because he wanted to sleep  in a real bed  from ages 4 to 16 bezos spent summers on  his grandparents ranch in texas doing  things like repairing windmills and  riding balls  bezos fell in love with reruns of the  original star trek and became a fan of  later versions too in school bezos told  teachers the future of mankind is not on  this planet even as a kid he wanted to  be a space entrepreneur  after spending a miserable summer  working at mcdonald's as a teen he  became obsessed with the idea of  managing employees  bezos together with his girlfriend  started the dream institute a 10-day  summer camp for kids they charged 600 a  kid and managed to sign up six students  bezos eventually went to college at  princeton university and majored in  computer science upon graduation he  turned down job offers from intel and  bell labs to join a new startup  after he quit he got a job at the hedge  fund d.e shore he became a senior vice  president after only four years in 1993  he married mackenzie tuttle a de shore  research associate a year later bezos  read that the web had grown 2 300 in one  year this number astounded him and he  decided he needed to find some way to  take advantage of this rapid growth  [Music]  [Applause]  he made a list of 20 possible products  to sell online and decided books were  the best option  bezos decided to leave d e shaw even  though he had a great job his boss at  the firm david e shaw tried to persuade  bezos to stay but bezos was already  determined to start his own company he  felt he'd rather try and fail at a  startup than never try at all on july  5th 1994 amazon was born jeff bezos and  his wife mackenzie opened an online  bookstore in seattle because of the  city's reputation as a tech hub and  small population they wouldn't have to  charge sales tax to most of their  customer base  bezos financed the company with ten  thousand dollars from his own pocket  he and a small team spent their early  days working in his garage on desks made  out of doors purchased from home depot  in the early days a bell would ring in  the office every time someone made a  purchase and everyone would gather  around to see whether anyone knew the  customer after a few weeks the bell was  ringing so often they had it removed  in the first month of its launch amazon  sold books to customers in all 50 states  and in 45 different countries  on may 15 1997 amazon went public at 18  per share giving it a valuation of 300  million dollars  in june 1998 amazon expanded its  offerings and started selling cds and  dvds the music section launched with 125  000 titles in september 1999 amazon  patented the ability to purchase an item  online with the click of a mouse called  one click the technology gave the  company an early advantage by promoting  customers to buy more and allow the  company to collect their data  amazon would later sue barnes noble for  infringement forcing other websites to  license the technology until the patent  expired in 2017. in november amazon  launched its third-party seller  marketplace that sparked a huge increase  in the volume of transactions on  amazon.com over the first four months a  quarter of a million customers bought  something using the service  in december 10th 1999 amazon took a hit  after the dot-com bubble burst  although it would survive the dot-com  crash amazon wasn't totally immune its  stock peaked at nearly 106 dollars a  share at the end of 1999 before taking a  long slide pummeled by negative analyst  reports  the company would lay off 15 of its  workforce before the stock price  recovered  in the same month time magazine named  bezos person of the year dubbing him the  king of cybercommerce  on june 10th 2003 amazon launched its  web hosting business  the company started to generate extra  income by licensing its platform  to other e-commerce sites like  borders.com and target.com  nowadays amazon web service continues to  dominate cloud hosting and is one of the  company's biggest revenue drivers  in august 2004 amazon entered china  amazon paid 75 million dollars to buy  yoyo the largest online seller of books  and electronics in the growing chinese  market  but the division renamed amazon china  faced stiff competition and was trailing  far behind alibaba in february 2005  amazon prime debuted bezos announced a  79 a year loyalty program that included  free two-day shipping on any order  now with more than 200 million members  worldwide  prime is considered one of amazon's most  valuable assets in november 2007 the  kindle went on sale the electronic  reading device enabled users to  wirelessly download books magazines and  newspaper articles among other content  in january 2008 amazon acquired audible  for 300 million dollars  audible now holds 41 of the audiobook  market which has been growing rapidly  with the rise of smartphones  in march 2012 amazon acquired robotics  company kiva systems for 775 million  dollars the deal allowed amazon to amp  up automation at its fulfillment centers  delivering products faster and with less  need for human staff amazon later  terminated kiva's client relationships  with retailers like office depot and the  gap in order to keep the technology to  themselves  in august 2013 bezos bought the  financially struggling washington post  for 250 million dollars  his plan was to take it private and pump  cash into new staff and better online  products  the newspaper now says it is profitable  in august 2014 amazon acquired the  social video game streaming site twitch  for 970 million dollars the deal not  only complemented the work being done at  amazon's own game production division  but it helped pull the global gaming  community towards its cloud platform  amazon web services  in june 2017 amazon acquired whole foods  after struggling for years in the  intensely competitive grocery delivery  business amazon upped the ante and  purchased whole foods  471 stores for 13.7 billion dollars  it has since started to integrate the  two companies distribution networks and  offer discounts to prime members in  september 2018 amazon reached a 1  trillion market cap in october 2020  amazon sales and profits surged during  the pandemic profits increased 197  percent to 6.3 billion revenue from  amazon web services the company's cloud  computing business and largest profit  driver grew 29  while its advertising business grew 51  in july 2021 jeff bezos hit a new wealth  record and became the richest man on the  planet with a net worth of  211 billion dollars  bezos has often come under fire about  his treatment of employees and other  controversies his former employees  describe bezos as a massive control  freak and amazon as a one-man show  former amazon engineer steve yegi said  bezos made ordinary control freaks look  like stoned hippies  bezos was known to tell his employees  that they should pay him to work at  amazon  in 2002 he demanded all his engineers to  rebuild their systems from the ground up  he also promised to fire every single  person who couldn't pull off this  massive undertaking he also brutalized  employees he had an array of harsh  insults he spewed at his employees with  such anger that blood vessels bulged on  his head amazon veterans have actually  collected a greatest hits list of  bezos's outbursts which range from why  are you wasting my life to labeling his  target incompetent or part of the bt  he's also not interested in charities  out of the four richest americans elon  musk bill gates mark zuckerberg and  himself he's the only one out of the  four who hasn't signed the giving pledge  a campaign to encourage extremely  wealthy people to contribute a majority  of their wealth to philanthropic causes  but in all fairness bezos has made some  charitable donations over the years in  2020 cbs news reported he launched the  bezos earth fund to help fight climate  change with a 10 billion dollar gift in  2018 he started the day one fund which  aims to devote two billion dollars to  address homelessness and education  issues  but it's still a minuscule amount  compared to the 41.3 billion bill gates  gave in charitable donations between  2000 and 2016. he's also a self-admitted  tax dodger bezos famously decided to  found amazon in washington state instead  of the hot tech hub of california and  was perfectly happy to admit that he did  it to dodge california's sales taxes  amazon's attitude toward taxes hasn't  exactly changed through the years as a  massive economic player it's able to  parlay governments and states for  billions and billions of tax deductions  in 2017 the company made 5.6 billion in  pure profit and in 2018 cnbc reported a  profit of 11 billion dollars yet amazon  paid no federal income taxes whatsoever  this gives them a price advantage over  traditional book retailers and help them  to establish a strong foothold in  e-commerce he's also known to pick on  smaller players in different markets  bezos ordered his employees to approach  small publishers the way a cheetah would  pursue a sickly gazelle amazon  executives took such sadistic pleasure  in abusing publishers that they named  its publisher abusing business program  the gazelle project he also made  employees work against each other  employees were actively encouraged to  bash each other's ideas during meetings  and the company's internal phone  directory even features instructions on  how to give secret feedback about their  co-workers to their bosses the system  was called the anytime feedback tool  using it to snitch on a fellow worker  was so simple that it even provided  sample text  the system made the work environment  extremely stressful for employees  amazon warehouses are often described as  prisons life in an amazon warehouse is  so hectic that workers don't have enough  time to eat or drink properly some of  the employees even peed in bottles and  left them on the shelves because the  performance targets were so high that  they didn't have time to take a bathroom  break  many employees complained that the  targets are too high surveillance is  constant the pay is inadequate and  workplace injuries are depressingly  common  he also came under fire for a texting  scandal on january 9 2019 jeff bezos  announced on twitter that he and his  wife of 25 years mackenzie bezos were  divorcing he had developed a  relationship with former tv anchor  lauren sanchez some even released  private photos and messages in which  bezos was said to flaunt his strangely  robotic sexting skills he supposedly  wrote to sanchez i love you alive girl  and i will show you with my body and my  lips and my eyes very soon in july 2019  a settlement was reached with mackenzie  receiving 38 billion dollars over the  next two years she donated billions to  charities across a variety of sectors  in 2021 mackenzie married science  teacher dan jewett in a statement jeff  bezos wished the newlyweds well saying  dan is such a great guy  on july 5th 2021 jeff bezos stepped down  as ceo of amazon to focus on his other  ventures  now more than 20 years after going  public amazon has a market cap of over  1.4 trillion dollars at the time of  making this video  bezos and sanchez have had a whirlwind  two years attending wimbledon together  yachting with other moguls and  celebrities and vacationing in san  tropez they even got to meet leonardo  dicaprio  he's still involved in important  initiatives at amazon but he plans to  spend more time on philanthropy  including the bezos earth fund and his  day one fund as well as his two other  major endeavors the washington post  which he purchased in 2013 and his  rocket company blue origin his first  space flight with blue origin was a  success he launched into sub-orbital  space with three other people on the  first crewed mission of the company's  new shepard vehicle a landmark moment  for the man and the space tourism  industry in recent years bezos and elon  musk have seen their respective net  worth spike the two moguls have  flip-flopped for the spot of the world's  richest person though it appears elon  musk is staying on top with a fortune of  over 200 billion dollars there's no  doubt that jeff bezos is incredibly  successful he went from flipping burgers  at mcdonald's to becoming the richest  man on the planet  what can we learn from bezos's rise to  the top  well the reason why amazon is so  successful is that bezos has an  obsession with customer experience  amazon selection ease of use low prices  and fast delivery times are unparalleled  and it's the reason why amazon dominates  the retail space and while most  businesses focus on a particular market  amazon is involved in a wide variety of  industries such as logistics streaming  cloud storage payments and the list goes  on they've built an entire ecosystem  that has made amazon insanely profitable  but by doing so they've compromised in  their treatment of employees and working  conditions by making customers their  number one priority amazon workers have  suffered due to the high expectations  and workload  some argue that bezos should be more  charitable while others argue that it's  his money and he can do whatever he  wants with it with regards to his tax  evasion critics often point out it's  unfair that he pays such little tax  while earning hundreds of millions per  day but some argue that big companies do  it all the time and if they do it  legally then they shouldn't owe the  government a penny is he a great  businessman who values his customers or  is he a real life lex luthor  the comment section in this one will be  very interesting  thanks for staying till the end of this  video if you enjoyed this video then be  sure to hit the like button drop a  comment subscribe and hit the  notification bell for more content like  this also be sure to check out our other  videos on this channel links are in the  description and we'll see you in the  next one
 

